  "response": "**Nothing in this PR should block the merge.** The scan reports 51 findings (3 high, 28 medium, 20 low), but no `agent_risk` score goes above 0.46. I opened the flagged code in the fixture, and many of the higher-scored findings turn out to be detector false positives.\n\n## False positives (ignore, and report to the tool)\n\n| IDs | Finding | sev / agent_risk | Why it's wrong |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 00003, 08, 15, 21, 26, 31, 38, 44 | `ts-imports.test.ts`: tests with no assertions | medium / 0.31 | Every test calls `checkClass()`, which runs two `expect(...).toEqual` checks (lines 9–12). The detector doesn't follow assertions into helper functions. |\n| 00004, 11, 18, 23, 29, 35, 42, 49 | `command.exitOverride.test.js`: tests with no assertions | medium / 0.27 | Every test ends with `expectCommanderError(...)`, which makes four `expect` calls. The file even says so at line 9: `eslint jest/expect-expect … assertFunctionNames: [\"expect\",\"expectCommanderError\"]`. The tool could read that eslint directive. |\n| 00006, 00007 | Docs-Code Drift: broken links in zh-CN docs | low / 0.24 | All three target files (`术语表.md`, `可变参数的选项.md`, `不再推荐使用的功能.md`) exist in `docs/zh-CN/`. The links are percent-encoded and the detector doesn't decode them before checking the disk. |\n| 00012 | Hardcoded `localhost:1234` in `lib/command.js:2152` | medium / 0.20 | It's in a comment (`// e.g. --inspect=localhost:1234`) next to the regex that parses `--inspect` flags. The code never connects to it. |\n\n## Real but harmless (ignore)\n\n| IDs | Finding | sev / agent_risk | Reasoning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 00001 | `\"PORT\"` literal in 5 files | medium / 0.46 (highest score) | Each example and type test is meant to stand alone, and `.env('PORT')` is the API being demonstrated. Pulling it into a shared constant would make the examples worse. |\n| 00002 | `myParseInt` duplicated in two examples | medium / 0.33 | Same reason: each example is copied into the README and has to run on its own. |\n| 00009, 16, 22, 27, 32, 39, 45, 10, 17 | Weak matchers (`toBeTruthy`/`toBeFalsy`) | low / 0.23 | The values under test are booleans (`_hasHelpOption`, `.variadic`), and each test checks both before and after. `toBe(true)` would be slightly stricter, but these tests do protect behaviour. |\n| 00005 | Rule hidden in a comment (signals test) | low / 0.27 | It's a historical note about SIGUSR1 and a debugger message, not a rule the code should enforce. The match came from words like \"required\" and \"retry\". |\n| 00014 | Commented-out code (search test) | low / 0.18 | The two lines document what the mocked `spawn` call looks like on each platform. They help the reader and aren't dead code. |\n| 00030, 37, 40, 41, 46 | Long `describe` callbacks or test functions | low–medium / 0.04–0.08 | These are long test suites, not complicated logic. |\n| 00047, 48, 50 | Long CHANGELOG and READMEs | low / 0.03–0.04 | That's normal for these documents. |\n| 00025 | `typings/index.test-d.ts` is 480 lines | medium / 0.11 | It's a type-test file, and its size follows the size of the API it tests. |\n\n## Warn (worth tracking, not a blocker)\n\n| IDs | Finding | sev / agent_risk | Reasoning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 00013, 00020 | `choices()` copied between `lib/argument.js:99-111` and `lib/option.js:176-188` | medium / 0.18 | This is real duplication in library code. A fix to the choices check, such as its error message or how it handles variadic values, has to be made in both places, and an agent could easily change only one. 00020 is just the inner part of 00013, so treat them as one item. |\n| 00024 | `lib/command.js` is 2179 lines (7.3× the threshold) | high / 0.11 | This is the main maintainability risk: an agent working on it won't have all of it in context. Severity is high but `agent_risk` is low because churn is 0 and the tests cover it well (test_gap 0). Track the split separately; don't hold this PR for it. |\n| 00028, 34, 43, 51 | Long functions: `_executeSubCommand` (120 lines), `parseOptions` (115), `_parseCommand` (80), `addOption` (69) | high/medium / 0.07–0.09 | They are real and tied to 00024. `_executeSubCommand` mixes several platform-specific spawn paths and is the best one to extract helpers from first. |\n| 00019 | `typings/index.d.ts` is 891 lines | high / 0.15 | A type declaration file that mirrors a large API. Its test_gap is 0.75, so agents editing types depend on `index.test-d.ts`. Keep the warning but give it low priority. |\n| 00033, 00036 | `lib/help.js` is 461 lines, `lib/option.js` is 326 | medium / 0.06–0.07 | Only slightly over the threshold. Warn without urgency. |\n\n## Notes for the scanner\n- 19 of the 51 findings are false positives, coming from three detector gaps: assertions made inside helper functions, percent-encoded doc links, and URLs that appear only in comments. Together they include 16 of the 28 medium findings and most of the top `agent_risk` scores. Fixing the `weak_test_signal` gap alone removes all 16.\n- The three \"high\" severity findings all have `agent_risk` of 0.15 or less, so severity and agent risk disagree here. Use `agent_risk` together with confidence and churn to rank findings, not severity alone.",
